{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בסכות תשבו שבעת ימים", :translit "b'sukot tishb'u shiv'at yamim", :gloss "in booths you shall dwell seven days"} {:he "ז' ענני כבוד", :translit "ziv ananei kavod", :gloss "seven clouds of glory"} {:he "ז' חופות", :translit "ziv chupot", :gloss "seven canopies"} {:he "ענן", :translit "anan", :gloss "cloud"} {:he "עשן", :translit "ashan", :gloss "smoke"} {:he "נוגה", :translit "nogah", :gloss "radiance"} {:he "אש", :translit "esh", :gloss "fire"} {:he "להבה", :translit "lehavah", :gloss "flame"} {:he "כבוד שכינה", :translit "kavod shechinah", :gloss "glory of the Shechinah"} {:he "וסכת לויתן", :translit "v'sukat livyatan", :gloss "and the booth of Leviathan"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Seven days of Sukkot correspond to seven clouds of glory and seven future canopies"}], :frame {:text "The clouds of glory protected Israel in the wilderness after the Exodus (Numbers 9:16–18). The tradition that righteous souls merit seven canopies (חופות) in the world to come appears in Sukkah 11b in the name of R. Abbahu, who lists the first six: cloud, smoke, radiance, fire, flame, and the glory of the Shechinah. The booth of Leviathan refers to the eschatological feast where the righteous will dwell in the canopy of Leviathan at the end of days (Bava Batra 74b, Leviathan is a designated food for the righteous in the future). The reasoning that Sukkot is observed at the onset of winter rather than summer so that the booth is visibly a mitzvah rather than mere shade reflects Talmudic discussion at Sukkah 28b–29a.", :sources ["Numbers 9:16–18" "Sukkah 11b" "Bava Batra 74b" "Sukkah 28b–29a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Seven days of Sukkot correspond to seven clouds of glory and seven future canopies", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:12.618948209Z", :model "glm-5.2"}